inct y, and distinct sums across its points. Is that possible? Let's try to construct for n=4.\n\nPoints list with (x,y) and sum s:\n\n(1,1) s=2\n(1,2) s=3\n(1,3) s=4\n(1,4) s=5\n(2,1) s=3\n(2,2) s=4\n(2,3) s=5\n(3,1) s=4\n(3,2) s=5\n(4,1) s=5\n\nWe need to assign each point to one of 4 lines L1-L4.\n\nConstraints: For each line, at most one point per column (x). So each line can have at most one point with x=1, at most one with x=2, etc.\n\nAlso at most one point per row (y) because if two points share same y, slope 0. So each line can have at most one point with y=1, y=2, etc.\n\nAlso at most one point per sum s because slope -1 would be formed if two points have same sum.\n\nThus each line must be a set of points with distinct x, distinct y, distinct s.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can have up to 4 points (since there are 4 columns). But total points are 10, so average 2.5 per line.\n\nWe need to assign points such that each line respects distinctness constraints.\n\nLet's try to see if possible.\n\nWe can think of constructing a 4x4 grid of possible points (x from 1 to 4, y from 1 to 4) but only those with x+y ≤5. So the shape is a right triangle.\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line. Let's try to assign points in column 1 (x=1) to each line: we have 4 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So each line must get exactly one of these. Let's assign:\n\nL1: (1,1) s=2\nL2: (1,2) s=3\nL3: (1,3) s=4\nL4: (1,4) s=5\n\nNow column 2 (x=2) has points (2,1) s=3, (2,2) s=4, (2,3) s=5. We need to assign each to a line, but each line can have at most one point from column 2. Also each line cannot have same y as already assigned point, and cannot have same sum as already assigned point.\n\nCheck each line's current y and sum:\n\nL1: y=1, s=2\nL2: y=2, s=3\nL3: y=3, s=4\nL4: y=4, s=5\n\nNow assign column 2 points:\n\n(2,1) y=1, s=3. y=1 already used by L1, so cannot assign to L1. s=3 already used by L2 (s=3). So cannot assign to L2. So possible lines: L3 (y=3, s=4) no conflict with y